  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWarm Saturday: Shortwave energy will move through the Rockies on\nSaturday with a front stretching from Iowa into Kansas. Latest\nensembles suggest that the daytime hours will be dry as forcing\nwill remain well west of the area. Therefore another warm and\ndry day looks likely with highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s.\nIf clouds move in quicker in the day then these temps could be\ntempered a bit. Low level moisture will slowly increase during\nthe day with mean dewpoints around 50 by late in the day as\nsoutherly winds increase. \n\nRain Chances Saturday Night/Sunday: Ensembles continue to\nsuggest that a neutrally tilted mid level trough will push into\nthe area late Saturday night into Sunday morning. The moisture\nand lift will likely force scattered showers along and behind \nthe front. Latest ensembles suggest the highest chances of \nrainfall will be early Sunday morning with a decreasing trend \nduring the day. The quick nature of the front and overall lack \nof widespread thunderstorms (other than a few isolated storms) \nwill limit the total rainfall amounts. That being said, most \nlocations look to see rain with this system. Latest NBM has a \n60-90% chance of 0.10in or greater and a 40-70% chance of 0.25in\nor greater. The chance of 1 inch or more of rain is less than \n10 percent. Overall highest rainfall amounts look to occur \nnorthwest of Springfield. If this system slows down and higher \nmoisture/instability occurs then rainfall amounts could \nincrease. High temps may be early in the day ahead of the front \nwith northerly post frontal winds ushering in cooler air during\nthe afternoon.\n\nCooler Temperatures Next Week: Cooler air aloft then looks to \nbuild in for early next week with temps closer to or slightly \nbelow average (highs in the 40s and lows in the 20s). Ensemble \nvariance/uncertainty increases with timing and location of \nenergy moving through this cooler airmass and precip chances are\nnow less than 10 percent through mid week.",
